      "text": "We start with\n\\[f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2.\\]We substitute $x = 3$ to get\n\\[f(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27.\\]We substitute $x = -1$ to get\n\\[f(-1) + 2f(-1) = 3(-1)^2.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[3f(-1) = 3,\\]which tells us that $f(-1) = 1.$\n\nWe substitute $x = -3$ to get\n\\[f(-3) + 2f((-3) - 3) = 3(-3)^2.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[f(-3) + 2f(-6) = 27.\\]We substitute $x = 3$ to get\n\\[f(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27.\\]We also substitute $x = -1$ to get\n\\[f(-1) + 2f(-1) = 3(-1)^2.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[3f(-1) = 3,\\]which tells us that $f(-1) = 1.$\n\nWe now have a system of two equations in two variables,\n\\[f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27,\\]and\\[f(-2) + 2f(-8) = 27.\\]By symmetry, we find that $-2 + (f(-2) + 2f(-8)) = 27,$ so we can substitute to get\n\\[f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27.\\]We subtract the two equations to get\n\\[f(3) - f(-2) = 0.\\]Therefore, we have $f(3) = f(-2).$  Let $f(3) = f(-2) = c.$  Then the two original equations become\n\\[c + 2c = 27,\\] which means $c = 9.$\n\nTherefore, $f(3) = \\boxed{9}.$. The answer is \\boxed{9}.",
