      "text": "To solve for \\( f(3) \\), we start with the given functional equation:\n\\[\nf(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's define \\( g(x) = f(x) + f(1 - x) \\). We will express everything in terms of \\( g(x) \\). From the given equation, we can substitute \\( y = 1 - x \\), which then gives \\( x = 1 - y \\). Euler's substitution for \\( f(x) + f(1 - x) = g(x) \\), with \\( f(1 - x) = f(y) \\), results in the equation:\n\\[\ng(x) + g(y) = 3x^2 + 3(1 - x)^2\n\\]\nExpanding \\( 3(1 - x)^2 \\) gives:\n\\[\n3(1 - x)^2 = 3(1 - 2x + x^2) = 3 - 6x + 3x^2\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\ng(x) + g(y) = 3x^2 + 3 - 6x + 3x^2\n\\]\nSimplify by combining like terms:\n\\[\ng(x) + g(y) = 6x^2 - 6x + 3\n\\]\nNow, we need to isolate \\( f(x) \\) and \\( f(1 - x) \\). To get closer to isolating \\( f(x) \\), we use \\( f(1 - x) = g(x) - f(x) \\):\n\\[\ng(x) + (f(1 - x) - f(1 - x)) = 3x^2 + 3\n\\]\nWhich simplifies to:\n\\[\ng(x) = 3x^2\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\ng(x) + g(1 - x) = 3x^2 + 3(1 - x)^2 = 3x^2 + 3 - 6x + 3x^2 = 6x^2 - 6x + 3\n\\]\nSince \\( g(x) = f(x) + f(1 - x) \\):\n\\[\n2f(x) = 6x^2 - 6x + 3 \\implies f(x) = 3x^2 - 3x + \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nNow, we determine \\( f(3) \\):\n\\[\nf(3) = 3(3)^2 - 3(3) + \\frac{3}{2} = 3(9) - 9 + \\frac{3}{2} = 27 - 9 + \\frac{3}{2} = 18 + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{36 + 3}{2} = \\boxed{\\frac{39}{2}}\n\\]",
